---
title: "Free Tool to Anonymize Chats With Fake Names"
description: "Replace every person in a chat or chat export with a consistent fake name. Works with WhatsApp, Slack, Teams and Discord. Runs in your browser."
faq:
  - q: "Is the Chat Text Anonymizer free?"
    a: "Yes. The tool is free, with no account, no sign-up and no limit on how often you use it."
  - q: "Is my chat uploaded to a server?"
    a: "No. The page has no server code. The tool reads your chat in your browser, and the text never leaves your device. If you turn on AI, the only download is the AI model itself, which contains no user data."
  - q: "How do I anonymize a WhatsApp chat?"
    a: "In WhatsApp, open the chat, choose Export chat and choose Without media. Open the exported text file (on iPhone, it is inside a .zip file), copy all the text and paste it into the Original pane. The tool finds each sender and gives them a fake name. Then copy the result or download it as a .txt file."
  - q: "Which chat apps does it support?"
    a: "The tool knows the WhatsApp export formats for iPhone and Android, and the text you get when you copy messages from Slack, Microsoft Teams and Discord. It also reads any chat written as Name: message lines, when two or more people write that way."
  - q: "Does the same person always get the same fake name?"
    a: "Yes. A full name, the first name alone and an @mention such as @sarah all get the same fake name. Each other person gets a different fake name, so you can still follow who said what."
  - q: "Is automatic anonymization 100% accurate?"
    a: "No automatic tool is perfect. Nicknames, misspelled names and details in the messages can still identify people. Turn on AI to find more names, read the result before you share it, and add any missed words to the always-hide list."
  - q: "Can someone undo the anonymization?"
    a: "No. In the text that you copy or download, the real names are replaced by fake names or labels. The original names are not stored in the text, so there is nothing to recover."
  - q: "Can I use it before I paste a chat into ChatGPT or another AI chatbot?"
    a: "Yes. Fake names keep the conversation natural, so the chatbot can still summarize or analyze it. If you want the chatbot to know the names are not real, choose numbered labels such as [NAME_1] instead."
